/**

@page documentation_tarch_visualisation_merging-data Merging data files

A parallel run writes one @ref tarch_patch_file_format "patch file" per
spacetree (and rank) and snapshot. merge.py glues the files of each snapshot
into one file. It yields few large files instead of many small ones, which is
handy to archive or to transfer data, and which makes
@ref documentation_tarch_visualisation_offline-conversion "render.py" and
@ref documentation_tarch_visualisation_probing-data "probe.py" open fewer files.
The script works with ASCII files and with files with binary quantities
(see @ref tarch_patch_file_format), and it does not rely on ParaView/pvpython or
vtk.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 -m tarch.plotter.api.merge --meta-file solution-Euler.peano-patch-file
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The script takes the meta file of a simulation, i.e. the file which links to
the snapshot files. For every snapshot, it writes a file
`merged_patch_file_<snapshot>.peano-patch-file` into the directory of the
meta file. It also writes `merged_meta_file.peano-patch-file`, which links to
the merged files. The latter file is a meta file like any other, i.e. you hand
it over to render.py or probe.py instead of the original meta file. The merged
files keep the format of the input: If you merge files with binary quantities,
you get files with binary quantities.

The options are

- **--meta-file** The meta file to merge (required).
- **-start/-end** Restrict the merge to a range of snapshots. Snapshots
  outside the range are skipped and do not show up in the new meta file.

## What the script does

The header of a merged file is the header of the first partial file. It
holds the format, the dimensions and the description of the data sets. The
script then appends all patches of all partial files of the snapshot. Patches
are copied byte by byte, i.e. the script does not parse the values and a merge
neither loses precision nor changes a file's format. All partial files of a
snapshot therefore have to have the same format and the same data sets, which
they do if they stem from one run.

 */
